    "subtitle": "Divide-and-conquer logarithmic search on sorted arrays",
    "answer": {
      "headline": "Binary search finds a target value in a sorted array in O(log n) time by continually halving the search window based on midpoint comparisons.",
      "body": [
        "Binary search operates on the principle of **decrease-and-conquer**. Unlike linear search, which inspects elements one by one from beginning to end, binary search requires the collection to be strictly sorted. It tracks two boundary pointers, `low` and `high`, defining the active search space.",
        "At each iteration, the algorithm calculates the midpoint `mid = low + (high - low) // 2`. Comparing the midpoint element `A[mid]` with the target yields one of three outcomes: an immediate match, or an elimination of half the remaining elements. If `target ‹ A[mid]`, the target cannot exist in the right half, so `high` moves to `mid - 1`. If `target › A[mid]`, `low` advances to `mid + 1`.",
        "Because each comparison discards exactly half of the remaining candidate indices, the search space shrinks as n, n/2, n/4, ..., 1. Consequently, even an array of one billion elements requires at most 30 comparisons to pinpoint the item or confirm it is absent."
      ],
      "caveats": [
        "Requires the underlying collection to be sorted and provide O(1) random memory access.",
        "Midpoint calculation in low-level languages must use `low + (high - low) / 2` to prevent 32-bit integer overflow."
      ]
    },
